7. In the Affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of the respondent No. 1, it is stated that the said 31 acres of land of plot No. 1 of Mouza Debpur, PS Kotwali, District, Sylhet was acquired in Land Acquisition Case No. 235 of 1930-31 for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the said Farm has been in possession of the said land since 1932. The said land was recorded as khas land by mistake in the khas khatian. The Khatian illegally prepared in the name of OJ Trust with respect to the land in question was cancelled by the Revenue Authority on 6-1-87. It is further stated and asserted that the Ministry of Land had no interest in the land in question as the same was acquired for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The Government in Memo No. V-239/84/559 dated 23-8-84 addressed to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the land was wrongly leased out to OJ Trust and asked the Deputy Commissioner to do the needful.&nbsp; 

 16. The admitted position is that 228 acres of land had been acquired in LA Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31 including 31 acres of land in question for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. The said Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm is admittedly under the control and management of the respondent No. 3, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock of the Government of Bangladesh. It is not denied by the petitioner that the said 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question had not been acquired for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. It is the positive common case set up by both the respondent Nos. 1 and 3 that Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm had been and has been in possession of the entire acquired 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question since the year 1932. The respondent No. 1, the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms in the affidavit-in-opposition categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm which is under the control of the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock. In the backdrop of this categorical assertion made by the respondent No. 1 as to the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s control over the land in question, the point became manifestly clear that it was only respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry who had the exclusive jurisdiction and competence to lease out the land in possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry did not have any authority, whatsoever, to lease out the disputed land in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust, merely because, they said land was not recorded in the name of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm but wrongly recorded in the khatian as Government khas land.&nbsp; 

 19. Annexure-A to the affidavit-in-opposition of the respondent No. 3 is a letter dated 7-5-1980 addressed to the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet. In the said letter, the aforesaid Enquiry Report of the Sub-Divisional Officer had been referred to. The Deputy Commissioner opined that the land in question was in possession of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. In the said letter the Deputy Commissioner stated that he for the first time came to know of the illegal settlement matter in favour of OJ Trust from the Superintendent of the Farm when he approached him and intimated of the alleged settlement. Thereafter, the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry made a series of letters and correspondences with the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry for cancelling the lease granted in favour of OJ Trust. It appears to us that in the backdrop of the letters and correspondences and the actual state of affairs, the Memo dated 21-5-1986 had been issued. Annexure-C to the writ petition is the Memo dated 21-5-1986 by which the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry cancelled the allotment in favour of petitioner OJ Trust with the decision that the Dairy Farm was a very important Government Project and no land of the Farm should be given to an individual on the plea that the same was recorded in collector&#39;&#39;s khatian as khas land and the Government Dairy Farm could not be penalised for wrong recording of the land as khas land. The Ministry in the said Memo asked the Deputy Commissioner to take appropriate step to revoke the lease deeds and to find out land elsewhere for settlement to OJ Trust and send proposal to the Ministry for consideration with copy to the petitioner. But neither the Deputy Commissioner nor the petitioner appears to have taken any further step in the matter.&nbsp;
